{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>Adonai Elohim.</b> The saying of <b>Adonai</b> follows what Eli said (I Samuel 3:18): ״אֲדֹנָי הוּא הַטּוֹב בְּעֵינָיו יַעֲשֶׂה״ — meaning, that the loss of the servant and his success affect the master, and since that is so — whatever is good in His eyes He will surely do. And his saying ״Adonai״ means: my success and my good are His." "And his saying YHWH with the vowels of Elohim — Rashi, of blessed memory, explained it, and this is his language: ״Adonai Elohim״ — merciful within judgment, end of quote. And I saw that the Ramban wrote regarding his words, and this is his language: ״And the Rav did not take note that the first Name is written with alef-dalet and the second is written with yod-heh, etc.״, end of quote. And I say that Rashi did not intend the attribute of mastery but rather the attribute of existence [HaVaYaH], for it is written YHWH and vowelized with the vowels of Elohim, and so are the words of the Midrash (Sifrei), and this is its language: YHWH — mercy; Elohim — judgment, end of quote. Meaning, the written form, which is YHWH — mercy; the reading, which is Elohim — judgment, and with this there is no difficulty." "<b>And the reason</b> he mentioned this Name that contains both mercy and judgment is according to what I explained in his saying ״leimor״ (to say), that his intention in his prayer was to pray also on behalf of Israel; therefore he mentioned judgment and mercy. Judgment — for that which concerns the nullification of the decree against Moses, as they said, of blessed memory (Tanchuma): A parable of a king who sought to marry a woman; he sent emissaries to see her, they went, etc. They came and said to him: ״There is none more blemished than her.״ The matchmaker heard, and said to him: ״My master, there is none more beautiful than her in the world.״ He came to marry her; the girl's father said to the king's emissaries: ״I swear that not one of you shall enter, since you disgraced her.״ The matchmaker came to enter; he said to him: ״You too shall not enter.״ The matchmaker said to him: ״I did not see her, and even so I said to the king: there is none more beautiful than her,״ etc. Thus Moses said before HaShem, etc., end of quote. You see that with a strong argument he comes to say that the line of judgment dictates that he shall surely ascend to the land — and this is what was intended by mentioning the Name Elohim. And he alluded, through the written form, to the Name of mercy, for that which concerns nullifying the decree from upon Israel, as I explained in the word ״leimor,״ and he was clever to mention the Name of judgment openly because in the revealed plane he prayed only for himself, while he alluded to the Name of mercy in the hidden plane, because the prayer of Israel was hidden within his prayer in potency, so that he would be answered for his own sake and Israel would be answered through him." "<b>Further,</b> it is possible that since he came with a claim to win justification according to the line of judgment, he was compelled to join the attribute of mercy — for who can be justified before the King of the universe with claims and counter-claims, and it is written (Psalms 143:2): ״כִּי לֹא יִצְדַּק לְפָנֶיךָ כָּל חָי״? Therefore he sweetened the judgment with the attribute of mercy." "<b>You have begun, etc.</b> Our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted the word ״הַחִלּוֹתָ״ in the Sifrei in three ways: the language of beginning, the language of prayer after the manner of his saying (Malachi 1:9) ״חַלּוּ נָא פְנֵי אֵל,״ and the language of release [from a vow]. And this is the language of the Sifrei: ״You have begun״ — You released my vow at the time You said to me (Exodus 3:10): ״הוֹצֵא אֶת עַמִּי,״ etc., and I said to You: I have already sworn to Yitro, etc. Another interpretation — You opened for me an opening to pray, for You said to me: ״הֶרֶף מִמֶּנִּי״ and the rest, and when had I grasped hold of You, etc. According to this, ״הַחִלּוֹתָ״ is interpreted in the language of prayer. Another interpretation — You began an opening at the time I entered the territories of the children of Gad and the children of Reuben, end of quote. It is difficult: well, the claim of ״You began״ and also the claim of ״You opened for me to pray״ are reasonable arguments for the prayer, but the claim of the opening of release — what proof does he bring from the fact that He released his oath to Yitro? The oath to Yitro is different, for it had an opening and was subject to release, and the opening is that the word of the supreme King came to him to go on His mission — and there is no greater opening than this. But as for his oath, may he be blessed, there is no valid opening for it, for all openings are revealed and foreseen before Him, and He declares the end from the beginning." "<b>And it may be explained</b> according to what Rambam wrote in chapter 6 of Hilkhot Shevuot, and this is his language: \"One who swore and did not retract, and comes to fulfill his oath — if the court sees that nullifying this oath [would serve] the fulfillment of a commandment or peace between a person and his fellow, etc., the court deliberates with him until he retracts, etc., and they permit it to him\" — end quote. And it implies that even though this opening is not an opening for him, i.e., a valid ground for nullification, nevertheless they employ stratagems upon him to bring him to retraction. And this is what Moses intended in his argument before HaShem: <b>You have begun</b> — that He permitted him his oath that he swore to Jethro, even though he [Moses] had not wished to permit it. And the proof is that for seven days HaShem said to him (Exodus 3:10): ״לְכָה וְאֶשְׁלָחֲךָ אֶל פַּרְעֹה״, and he did not want to, for whatever reason, and HaShem deliberated with him until he was appeased for the sake of the commandment, and he went and permitted his vow. And likewise HaShem will permit the oath of the decree against Moses, and even though there is no opening here, there is here a matter of commandment — to show him the good land, as he said: ״אֶעְבְּרָה נָּא וְאֶרְאֶה״ etc." "And if you say: what comparison is there between the law Rambam wrote and the question of Moses? For Rambam's law that permits is [in a case where] the one who swore is the one who fulfills the commandment, and therefore they permit him; but in our case HaShem swore, and by its nullification Moses is the one who performs the commandment of going to the land. To this Moses was precise in his words and said <b>Your servant</b> — and it is known that whatever a servant acquires, his master acquires."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {2 "The parable from Tanchuma is truncated in the source with וכו׳ (etc.); rendered as such."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:15:09.095633249Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}